{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014VaHYc77zeW8bpRppniCeY","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd027-paste\n     2→title: \"cmd/paste: Merge Lines of Files Side by Side\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS BSD paste is functionally equivalent to GNU paste for common usage, but there are\n     6→  edge-case differences in delimiter cycling behavior and the treatment of exhausted files.\n     7→  Scripts that merge columnar data from multiple files depend on byte-exact output. The Go\n     8→  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gpaste, installed by\n     9→  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n    10→  pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Read one line from each file in turn and write them as a single output line separated by a delimiter, matching GNU paste behavior.\n    14→  - G2: Support -d to configure the delimiter or delimiter list used between fields.\n    15→  - G3: Support -s to paste one file at a time in serial mode (all lines of one file on one output line).\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Default parallel merge\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: Must open all named files simultaneously and read one line from each per output line. Lines from files are joined with the delimiter and written to stdout, followed by a newline.\n    22→      - R1.2: When files have unequal line counts, the shorter files contribute empty fields (the delimiter is still written) until all files are exhausted. The final output line ends with a newline.\n    23→      - R1.3: stdin is used as an input when \"-\" is given as a filename. Multiple \"-\" arguments each refer to stdin read sequentially.\n    24→      - R1.4: When no files are given, must read from stdin and write each line followed by a newline (passthrough behavior matching gpaste with a single stdin source).\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Delimiter configuration (-d)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -d DELIM must use DELIM as the separator between fields. DELIM may be a single character or a list; if a list is given, the delimiters are cycled in order across the fields of each output line.\n    30→      - R2.2: The escape sequences \\\\n (newline), \\\\t (tab), \\\\\\\\ (backslash), and \\\\0 (empty string, no delimiter) are recognized in DELIM.\n    31→      - R2.3: When DELIM cycles back to the start for a new output line, cycling resets from the first delimiter in the list.\n    32→\n    33→  R3:\n    34→    title: Serial mode (-s)\n    35→    items:\n    36→      - R3.1: -s must process files one at a time. All lines of the first file are joined with the delimiter and written as a single output line. Then all lines of the second file are written as a single output line, and so on.\n    37→      - R3.2: In serial mode, the delimiter list cycles across fields within the single output line for each file. The trailing newline is always written.\n    38→      - R3.3: -s and the default parallel mode are mutually exclusive; -s overrides parallel mode.\n    39→\n    40→  R4:\n    41→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    42→    items:\n    43→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    44→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing halts on the first error.\n    45→      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    46→      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    47→\n    48→non_goals:\n    49→  - cmd/paste does not buffer entire files in memory; it reads one line per file per output line in parallel mode.\n    50→  - cmd/paste does not implement Unicode multi-byte delimiter expansion beyond the recognized escape sequences.\n    51→\n    52→acceptance_criteria:\n    53→  - \"AC1: paste with two files containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' and '1\\\\n2\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\t1\\\\nb\\\\t2\\\\n', matching gpaste.\"\n    54→  - \"AC2: paste -d: with the same files produces 'a:1\\\\nb:2\\\\n', matching gpaste -d:.\"\n    55→  - \"AC3: paste -s with a file containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc\\\\n', matching gpaste -s.\"\n    56→  - \"AC4: paste with files of unequal length produces correct output with empty fields for exhausted files, matching gpaste.\"\n    57→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gpaste passes for all required test cases.\"\n    58→  - \"AC6: cmd/paste compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    59→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cc92e92-4320-4730-860e-38fed7bca8cc","uuid":"ca234bc4-e41d-4446-8444-c776525fe9d2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/355/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd027-paste.yaml","content":"id: prd027-paste\ntitle: \"cmd/paste: Merge Lines of Files Side by Side\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD paste is functionally equivalent to GNU paste for common usage, but there are\n  edge-case differences in delimiter cycling behavior and the treatment of exhausted files.\n  Scripts that merge columnar data from multiple files depend on byte-exact output. The Go\n  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gpaste, installed by\n  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Read one line from each file in turn and write them as a single output line separated by a delimiter, matching GNU paste behavior.\n  - G2: Support -d to configure the delimiter or delimiter list used between fields.\n  - G3: Support -s to paste one file at a time in serial mode (all lines of one file on one output line).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default parallel merge\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must open all named files simultaneously and read one line from each per output line. Lines from files are joined with the delimiter and written to stdout, followed by a newline.\n      - R1.2: When files have unequal line counts, the shorter files contribute empty fields (the delimiter is still written) until all files are exhausted. The final output line ends with a newline.\n      - R1.3: stdin is used as an input when \"-\" is given as a filename. Multiple \"-\" arguments each refer to stdin read sequentially.\n      - R1.4: When no files are given, must read from stdin and write each line followed by a newline (passthrough behavior matching gpaste with a single stdin source).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Delimiter configuration (-d)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -d DELIM must use DELIM as the separator between fields. DELIM may be a single character or a list; if a list is given, the delimiters are cycled in order across the fields of each output line.\n      - R2.2: The escape sequences \\\\n (newline), \\\\t (tab), \\\\\\\\ (backslash), and \\\\0 (empty string, no delimiter) are recognized in DELIM.\n      - R2.3: When DELIM cycles back to the start for a new output line, cycling resets from the first delimiter in the list.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Serial mode (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: -s must process files one at a time. All lines of the first file are joined with the delimiter and written as a single output line. Then all lines of the second file are written as a single output line, and so on.\n      - R3.2: In serial mode, the delimiter list cycles across fields within the single output line for each file. The trailing newline is always written.\n      - R3.3: -s and the default parallel mode are mutually exclusive; -s overrides parallel mode.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing halts on the first error.\n      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/paste does not buffer entire files in memory; it reads one line per file per output line in parallel mode.\n  - cmd/paste does not implement Unicode multi-byte delimiter expansion beyond the recognized escape sequences.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: paste with two files containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' and '1\\\\n2\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\t1\\\\nb\\\\t2\\\\n', matching gpaste.\"\n  - \"AC2: paste -d: with the same files produces 'a:1\\\\nb:2\\\\n', matching gpaste -d:.\"\n  - \"AC3: paste -s with a file containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc\\\\n', matching gpaste -s.\"\n  - \"AC4: paste with files of unequal length produces correct output with empty fields for exhausted files, matching gpaste.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gpaste passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/paste compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":59,"startLine":1,"totalLines":59}}}
